<BaseLayout title="About" description='A bit about me, and why "Without Effort" exists.' lang="en">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>My name is Matan.</p>

    <p>
      I didn't come to Buddhism because I was looking for a philosophy.
      I came because life stopped making sense in the ways I was used to
      understanding it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Over the years, Zen has become less about finding answers and more
      about learning how to stay with the questions.
    </p>

    <p>This website is a collection of that ongoing practice.</p>

    <p>
      I write about Zen, meditation, impermanence, grief, identity, and
      the ordinary moments where practice quietly meets everyday life.
      Sometimes those reflections come from sitting on a cushion.
      Sometimes they come from raising children, navigating loss, or
      simply noticing the mind trying to hold on to something that is
      already changing.
    </p>

    <p>
      I don't consider myself a teacher. I'm a student who has found
      value in sharing the path as honestly as I can.
    </p>

    <p>
      Many of the ideas here are inspired by Soto Zen, the writings of
      teachers who have influenced me, and my own experience of
      practice. They are not presented as truths to believe, but as
      invitations to investigate for yourself.
    </p>

    <p>If there is a common thread running through everything on this site, it is simple:</p>

    <p><em>How do we meet this moment without needing it to be different?</em></p>

    <p>I'm still learning.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
